From: steamac <steamac@fxgam.es>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 13:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] kk: split Metal command buffers every 4096 encoders

STEAMAC-G/CT (1.9.15, M4 Pro 24 GB, Black Mesa's native Linux renderer
through Zink): "kk: it failed with OutOfMemory and had 30721 render passes,
961 compute encoders, 30720 draws (30720 indirect) ... 1 Metal command
buffers", 0.0 ms on the GPU, then device loss and the game's abort. Two
events, the same shape.

Metal 4 fails a command buffer with too many render encoders at commit and
nothing in it runs; vkQueueWaitIdle still succeeds, the failure only shows
in the commit feedback. On an M1 Max one command buffer runs 36,864
clear-only render passes (6.4 GB of allocator storage) and fails at 38,912
(6.8 GB); 20,000 render passes with 20,000 compute encoders between them
run. The M4 Pro failed lower, with ~2 GiB of storage: not a byte budget.

Before a new render or compute encoder of the main Metal command buffer,
once it has 4096 encoders, the recording goes on in a new Metal command
buffer on the same allocator, the way timestamp writes already split
(0064, which now shares the switch). Only between encoders and never with a
pre_gfx pass open: every encoder ends with a barrier after all its stages
before all later queue stages, later command buffers included. One extra
Metal command buffer (~55 us of GPU time) per 4096 encoders.

Not addressed: Foundation's 45.5 GiB of allocator storage over 16,384
pre_gfx-split Metal command buffers of 1-2 encoders each (STEAMAC-S).

Regression: host/moltenvk/repro/sentry-ct-encoders.c (REPRO_CASE=
sentry-ct-encoders): 40,000 clear-only render passes and 40,000 dependent
buffer copies in one command buffer. 1.9.15 runs none of it (OutOfMemory);
after, every copy sees the one before it.

Generated-by: LLM
---
 src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c | 53 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 44 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c
index e34d243..0fa3747 100644
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_cmd_buffer.c
@@ -440,6 +440,26 @@ kk_cmd_next_cmd_buf_after_pre_gfx(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd)
    cmd->metal.ts_writes = 0;
 }
 
+/* The recording goes on in `next`, a new Metal command buffer on the same
+ * allocator. Only between encoders: every encoder ends with a barrier after
+ * all its stages before all later queue stages, later command buffers
+ * included, so the split keeps the order. */
+static void
+kk_cmd_switch_cmd_buf(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd, mtl_command_buffer *next)
+{
+   assert(cmd->metal.render == NULL && cmd->metal.compute == NULL &&
+          cmd->metal.pre_gfx.cmd_buf == NULL);
+   mtl_end_command_buffer(cmd->metal.cmd_buf);
+   mtl_begin_command_buffer(next, cmd->metal.allocator);
+   if (cmd->vk.base.object_name)
+      mtl_command_buffer_set_label(next, cmd->vk.base.object_name);
+   util_dynarray_append(&cmd->metal.cmd_bufs, next);
+   cmd->metal.cmd_buf = next;
+   cmd->metal.cmd_buf_used = false;
+   cmd->metal.ts_writes = 0;
+   cmd->metal.encoder_count = 0;
+}
+
 /* About 4x below the M5 Pro's measured limit of 61,680 single-entry resolves.
  * M1 Max survives 40,000 such writes but fails at 65,536. */
 #define KK_TS_WRITES_PER_CMD_BUF 16384u
@@ -458,15 +478,28 @@ kk_cmd_count_ts_writes(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd, uint32_t writes)
       return;
 
    cs_end(cmd);
-   mtl_end_command_buffer(cmd->metal.cmd_buf);
-   mtl_begin_command_buffer(next, cmd->metal.allocator);
-   if (cmd->vk.base.object_name)
-      mtl_command_buffer_set_label(next, cmd->vk.base.object_name);
-   util_dynarray_append(&cmd->metal.cmd_bufs, next);
-   cmd->metal.cmd_buf = next;
-   cmd->metal.cmd_buf_used = false;
-   cmd->metal.ts_writes = 0;
-   cmd->metal.encoder_count = 0;
+   kk_cmd_switch_cmd_buf(cmd, next);
+}
+
+/* Metal 4 fails a command buffer with too many encoders at commit with
+ * OutOfMemory, and nothing in it runs (STEAMAC-G/CT: Black Mesa's 30,721
+ * render passes, 961 compute encoders and 30,720 indirect draws, M4 Pro 24 GB,
+ * ~2 GiB of allocator storage). An M1 Max runs 36,864 clear-only render
+ * passes (6.4 GB) and fails at 38,912 (6.8 GB), so it is not a byte budget.
+ * A Metal command buffer costs ~55 us of GPU time; one per 4096 encoders is
+ * noise next to the encoders themselves. */
+#define KK_ENCODERS_PER_CMD_BUF 4096u
+
+/* Before a new encoder of cmd_buf, none open */
+static void
+kk_cmd_split_if_full(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd)
+{
+   if (cmd->metal.encoder_count < KK_ENCODERS_PER_CMD_BUF)
+      return;
+
+   mtl_command_buffer *next = kk_cmd_pool_get_cmd_buf(kk_cmd_buffer_pool(cmd));
+   if (next != NULL)
+      kk_cmd_switch_cmd_buf(cmd, next);
 }
 
 /* A Metal command buffer of its own for the pre_gfx pass of the open render
@@ -506,6 +539,7 @@ cs_start_render(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd)
 
    /* Ensure no other active encoder, aka compute */
    cs_end(cmd);
+   kk_cmd_split_if_full(cmd);
 
    /* A rendering whose draws recorded pre_gfx work (cs_get_pre_gfx) starts
     * its render encoders that would follow other encoders in a Metal command
@@ -592,6 +626,7 @@ cs_get_compute(struct kk_cmd_buffer *cmd)
    }
 
    if (cmd->metal.compute == NULL) {
+      kk_cmd_split_if_full(cmd);
       cmd->metal.encoder_count++;
       kk_cmd_note_storage(cmd, "compute", false);
       cmd->metal.compute = mtl_new_compute_command_encoder(cmd->metal.cmd_buf);
